{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["U26", "U6", "U29", "R43", "R25", "R28"],
      "pins": ["U26.1", "U26.2", "U6.1", "U6.2", "U29.1", "U29.2"],
      "nets": ["NET0014", "NET0174", "NET0048", "GND"],
      "observed": "The e-stop control signals NET0014, NET0174 and NET0048 are connected to pin 1 (NC) of the SN74LVC1G07 buffers U26, U6 and U29, while the actual input pin 2 of each buffer is tied directly to GND.",
      "expected": "The control signals should drive the buffer inputs on pin 2, with pin 1 left unconnected, so that the open-drain outputs can sink the e-stop lines under control.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Tile 9: U26 pin 1 connects to NET0014, pin 2 connects to GND; U6 pin 1 connects to NET0174, pin 2 connects to GND; U29 pin 1 connects to NET0048, pin 2 connects to GND."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Safety/e-stop: an e-stop loop with multiple sink points (ESTOP_SINK1, ESTOP_SINK2, ESTOP_SINK_LOCAL) gated through single non-inverting buffers (SN74LVC1G07, parts U15, U22, U35, U36) wired as /OE-controlled sinks, feeding an ESTOP_OUT signal."}
      ],
      "impact": "The buffer inputs are permanently low, so their open-drain outputs are always asserted. The e-stop sink is continuously active and the control signals have no effect, preventing normal robot operation.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R62", "R64", "R97", "U46"],
      "pins": ["U46.4", "R62.1", "R62.2", "R64.1", "R64.2", "R97.1", "R97.2"],
      "nets": ["VMOT", "+3V3", "GND", "AIN0"],
      "observed": "The input-voltage sense divider is wired as R62 50K from VMOT to AIN0, R64 50K from AIN0 to +3V3, and R97 5.1K from +3V3 to GND. AIN0 therefore sits at approximately (VMOT + 3.3V)/2.",
      "expected": "For sensing VMOT with the ADS1115, AIN0 should be a divider from VMOT to GND, with resistor values chosen so that the maximum VMOT produces less than 3.3V at AIN0.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Tile 6: VMOT -> R62 50K 0.1% -> AIN0 (U46 pin 4); AIN0 -> R64 50K 0.1% -> +3V3; +3V3 -> R97 5.1K 0.1% -> GND."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Changelog: 'Add I2C ADC for input voltage sensing' and U46 is an ADS1115IDGS I2C ADC."}
      ],
      "impact": "With a 24V VMOT, AIN0 would be about 13.6V, far above the ADS1115 supply and absolute maximum ratings. The ADC can be damaged and the input voltage reading will be incorrect.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U2", "U19"],
      "pins": ["U2.5", "U2.8", "U19.5", "U19.8"],
      "nets": ["GND", "5V_switched_CAN"],
      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U2 and U19 have their bus-side ground pins GND2 tied to the main GND net and their bus-side supply pins VCC2 tied to 5V_switched_CAN, which is derived from the non-isolated +5V rail.",
      "expected": "For isolated CAN buses, the bus side should be powered from an isolated 5V supply and referenced to an isolated ground, not to the main GND and +5V rails.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Tile 7: U2 pin 5 (GND2) connects to GND, pin 8 (VCC2) connects to 5V_switched_CAN. Tile 10: U19 pin 5 (GND2) connects to GND, pin 8 (VCC2) connects to 5V_switched_CAN."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Motor/actuator interfaces: two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller through an isolated CAN transceiver."}
      ],
      "impact": "The galvanic isolation of both CAN buses is defeated. Ground loops and common-mode noise can couple between the CM4 and the motor controllers, and the safety isolation intended by the design is lost.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic contains several deviations from the intended design: the e-stop buffer inputs are grounded while the control signals are on NC pins, the input-voltage sense divider is miswired and can overvoltage the ADC, and the isolated CAN transceivers are connected to non-isolated ground and power, defeating their isolation."
}

